    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4276",
      "problem": "The vectors $\\textbf{a},$ $\\textbf{b},$ and $\\textbf{c}$ are shown in the diagram below. Find $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}-\\textbf{c}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/9665262608454d38d6a442715345cc014d40ca2e3d9a7cdf7c8cd0b8fc00f0d7.png)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThe resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that joins the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ and the head of $\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/2643b94a575f1b2cd9967e530019b8b100928b2f9888aaf833dd84423fbe6e10.png)\n\nNow, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n(\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b})+(-\\textbf{c}).\n$$\n\nWe draw $-\\textbf{c}$ from the head of $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}.$ So, we have the vector that goes from the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\textbf{c}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/d77ad19b87fe8afba44663482ed6e8adfb2f251c4439c488ab7b3cde8b4a60ac.png)",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/observations/step-e4276-3389055937b9a7edf1e9.html",
